WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:07.389
Hi everyone and welcome to Biology
Professor. Today we're going to be talking

00:00:07.389 --> 00:00:13.300
about the Gram Stain procedure. The Gram Stain is a very commonly used method in

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:18.400
laboratory and medical settings. It is
called a differential stain, so that

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:23.110
means that it helps someone to
differentiate or distinguish between two

00:00:23.110 --> 00:00:27.400
different classes of bacteria,
specifically gram positive bacteria and

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:31.210
gram-negative bacteria. For more
information on the differences between

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:35.589
those two types of bacteria, see my video
on gram positive and gram negative

00:00:35.589 --> 00:00:40.289
bacteria. Today we're going to talk about
the steps of the Gram Stain.

00:00:40.289 --> 00:00:46.870
It starts with heat fixing a sample. This
is when you have a biological sample

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:52.569
that has been heat fixed to a slide
simply by passing it over a flame. This

00:00:52.569 --> 00:00:58.149
helps the sample containing all of the
bacteria to stick to the slide through

00:00:58.149 --> 00:01:03.159
all the subsequent steps. The second step is the use of crystal

00:01:03.159 --> 00:01:13.150
violet dye. This is a purple dye that
stains all of the bacteria. So you can

00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:18.220
see here that after we use this crystal
violet dye, all of the bacteria in the

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:24.790
sample are going to stain purple. This is
because crystal violet dye associates

00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:29.590
with peptidoglycan and all bacteria have
at least some peptidoglycan in their

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:40.240
cell walls.  Now crystal violet is a
positively (+) charged molecule and iodine

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:46.080
is a negatively (-) charged molecule. This
means that together they form a complex.

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:50.970
Iodine for this reason is called the
mordant.

00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:59.020
It helps the crystal violet to link to
the peptidoglycan in gram positive cell

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:05.680
walls because they have a much thicker
layer of peptidoglycan. Then these cells,

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:10.360
after they've been treated with the
crystal violet primary stain and the

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:13.690
iodine mordant,
they are treated with a decolorizing

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:18.070
solution but the decolorizing solution, it
is usually some form of alcohol

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:24.489
that helps the crystal violet and iodine
to be washed away from cells where they

00:02:24.489 --> 00:02:28.239
aren't very strongly adhering. These
would be the gram-negative cell walls

00:02:28.239 --> 00:02:32.560
that only have a very, very thin layer
of peptidoglycan, leaving the

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:36.670
crystal violet dye and the iodine in the
gram-positive cell walls, which have that

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:42.900
much thicker layer of peptidoglycan. So
after using this decolorizing solution,

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:53.519
you have the gram positive cells still
retaining that purple crystal violet dye

00:02:54.340 --> 00:03:01.870
and then you have gram-negative cells
that are suddenly colorless again. So

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:07.060
that is from the decolorizing solution.
At this point, you can visualize which

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:11.470
cells are gram positive because they
have retained this crystal violet dye

00:03:11.470 --> 00:03:17.080
after using the decolorizing solution, but
then the final step is the use of a

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:22.180
counter stain, safranin, which has a pink
color just to help distinguish further

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:29.380
between those two cell types. So after
the use of safranin, crystal violet is

00:03:29.380 --> 00:03:35.980
still in all of the gram-positive cell
walls so that these are all still purple

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:41.080
stained and you can distinguish that
they are gram positive, whereas the

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:49.530
gram-negative cell walls have taken up
this safranin counterstain making them pink.

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:56.799
Now at the end of a procedure like this,
you can see how it's fast, it's

00:03:56.799 --> 00:04:02.170
inexpensive, it's relatively simple and
one of the reasons it's so useful in a

00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:06.010
laboratory or hospital setting is it
helps to give what's called a

00:04:06.010 --> 00:04:11.860
presumptive diagnosis.
We see that the majority of cells in the

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:18.459
sample are gram positive rods, so
purple gram positive rod-shaped bacteria.

00:04:18.459 --> 00:04:27.190
The most common, or one of the most
common causes of hospital-acquired

00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:33.100
diarrhea in adults is called
Clostridium difficile, and it is a gram

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:46.570
positive rod-shaped bacterium. It is the
most likely cause of the infection that

00:04:46.570 --> 00:04:54.730
was take the sample was taken from.  And then knowing this information not only

00:04:54.730 --> 00:04:58.840
do we have a presumptive diagnosis that
can then be confirmed through later

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:04.410
studies, we also know what kind of
bacteria we're working with, which helps

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:09.580
doctors to prescribe the correct
antibiotic regimen to treat this

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:13.250
infection and so this is why a Gram
Stain is so important and

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:16.090
this is how it works.

